// **The five built-in traits with no trait object are refused in every type position**, not only
// at a parameter.
//
// `dyn Name` reaches the type system through one door, so a trait with no object should be refused
// wherever the words are written. "Should" is the operative word: the door is one function, and a
// position that forgets to call it names a type nothing can inhabit and reports nothing —
// silently, because the annotation then simply never matches anything. The companion case
// `dyn_builtin_trait_without_object` pins the refusal itself at a parameter; this pins that the
// door is reached from every other position an annotation can occupy.
//
// One trait per position rather than five in each: the positions are what is being covered, and
// the trait-by-trait half is already asserted next door. The `dyn` column of the built-in table is
// restated a third time in `noeta-types`' own census, so widening the set costs three edits.
// expect: error E0014 at 24:18
// expect: error E0014 at 25:15
// expect: error E0007 at 25:33
// expect: error E0014 at 26:8
// expect: error E0007 at 26:17
// expect: error E0014 at 27:14
// expect: error E0014 at 28:21
// expect: error E0014 at 29:19
// expect: error E0014 at 30:20
// expect: exit 1
struct Held { c: dyn Clone }
fn returns(): dyn From { return 1 }
bound: dyn To = 1
listed: List<dyn Serialize> = []
mapped: Map<string, dyn Deserialize> = {}
fn nested(x: List<dyn Clone>): int { return 1 }
fn tupled(x: (int, dyn Serialize)): int { return 1 }
echo 0
